# --- Sign-on ------------------------------------------------------------------
::lines intro
Good evening, wasteland, and welcome to DEADBALL. Chip Vega in the booth, and we've got nine innings of ball for whoever's still got working eyes.
Chip Vega here at the ballpark, where the lights work maybe sixty percent of the time and the hot dogs are legally a dare. Let's play ball.
This is DEADBALL on Coldwater League radio. I'm Chip Vega. Somewhere out there the sun's setting on a stadium that has definitely, definitely been condemned.
Welcome back to the ballgame. Chip Vega, and I'll be your only friend for the next nine innings.
Good evening from the Charles B. Hughson Coliseum. Chip Vega in the booth, and Sir Charles is out front in bronze, same as every night.
::endlines

::lines matchup
Tonight it's the {away} in from out of town to face the {home} on their home dirt.
On the marquee: {away} versus {home}. Two rosters, one working scoreboard, no medical staff.
The {away} take the field against the {home}. Bad blood, worse plumbing. Here we go.
It's {home} hosting {away} under whatever's left of the stadium lights.
::endlines

# --- Matchup WITH standings (used once the league has records on the board) ------
::lines matchup.records
Tonight the {away}, {awayRecord} on the season, roll into town to face the {home} at {homeRecord}.
On the marquee: {away} ({awayRecord}) versus {home} ({homeRecord}). Somebody's record gets worse tonight.
The {away} bring their {awayRecord} mark in against the {home}, sitting at {homeRecord}. Standings on the line, such as they are.
It's {home} ({homeRecord}) hosting {away} ({awayRecord}), two clubs clawing up a league table nobody escapes.
::endlines

# --- Half-inning framing ------------------------------------------------------
::lines half.top
Top of the {inningOrd}. The {team} step up to hit.
Here's the top of the {inningOrd}. {team} coming to bat, the home nine taking the field.
Top {inningOrd}. {team} due up. Let's see who's swinging.
::endlines

::lines half.bottom
Bottom of the {inningOrd}. The home side, the {team}, grab the bats.
Middle of the {inningOrd} behind us, and now the {team} come up for their cuts.
Bottom {inningOrd}, {team} at the plate.
::endlines

# --- Booth chatter: filler BETWEEN outs — weather, the stadium, a raunchy aside,
# --- an obscure stat, or something out of left field. Baseball is long and dry;
# --- Chip fills the dead air. No score/plays here, pure colour. --------------------
::lines chatter
Not much doing out here. Wind's picking up off the flats. Smells like a battery fire, which, to be fair, it usually is.
You can see the haze rolling in over the left-field wall. They painted a distance marker on it once. Nobody's ever reached it. Nobody ever will.
Fun fact: this ballpark was built on a landfill, and on humid nights you can still smell every decision that led us here.
{pitcher} out there on the mound looking like a man who owes money to everyone in this stadium. Which, in fairness, he might.
The grounds crew tried to reseed the infield last spring. It came up brown and angry and now they just rake it and apologize.
I'm told the record for longest game at this park is fourteen hours. Seven people finished it. Two of them on purpose.
Somebody in the bleachers just took their shirt off. It's nine degrees out. I'm not going to editorialize, but I have concerns.
Weather's holding, which around here means the acid's only a light drizzle. Bring an umbrella, lose an umbrella. That's the deal.
They sell a hot dog here called the Widowmaker. It comes with a waiver. I've had three.
Little-known stat: no left-handed batter has hit safely at this park after sundown since the lights started flickering. Draw your own conclusions.
The {team} bullpen's been quiet. Then again, half of them are asleep and one of them, I believe, is technically deceased.
Quiet moment out here. Good time to mention the bathrooms are out of order. All of them. Have been since the last administration.
You know, my ex-wife loved this game. Sat right there in section nine. Section nine is a crater now. Life comes at you fast.
Ballpark organist is playing something in a minor key again. He only knows the one song. It's about drowning.
Vendor just walked past selling something he called "meat adjacent." I didn't follow up. I value what's left of me.
The stadium clock froze at 6:66 four years ago and honestly it's never been wrong about the mood.
Somewhere out past the outfield wall a dog is howling in perfect rhythm with the crowd. Nobody brought a dog.
They retired a number here once. Not for a player. For a guy who survived a full season in the cheap seats. Brave soul.
Folks ask me who Sir Charles was. He owned this ground. Halcyon offered him a fortune for it three times, and three times he sent the cheque back with two tickets in the envelope.
People rub the shoes on the Sir Charles statue out front for luck. The shoes are gold now. The luck's still bronze.
They keep Sir Charles's seat behind home plate empty. Nobody sits in it. Not even the drunks, and that's saying something.
Halcyon built a whole estate round this ballpark because Sir Charles wouldn't sell. You can still see the offices sulking over the left-field wall.
The Basin Blue Birds are the oldest club in this league. They were playing ball in Old Coldwater when the Meridian still had a doorman.
The Coldwater Crows go back to 2022, before the Curtain went up. One club in this league is older, and the Crows have spent every season since trying to bury it.
Blue Birds and Crows. Oldest grudge in the Basin. Nobody remembers what started it, and everybody remembers whose side they're on.
Sir Charles was a Blue Birds man. Never missed a home game, never said a kind word about the Crows, and the Crows have never said one back.
Never been a wire in a Crows uniform. Not one augment, not one graft, not since 2022. Ask them why and they just smile at you. Politely. For a long time.
The Crows don't sign free agents. Somebody vouches for you, you sit a season in the stands, and one day there's a jersey in your locker. Nobody ever says who left it.
You'll see crows on the light towers on game nights. Just sitting. Watching. The Crows' people say that's lucky. They don't say for who.
::endlines

# =============================================================================
# THE BOOTH BETWEEN THE PLAYS (plugins/broadcast/sports/baseball-booth.js)
# Chip Vega calls it and Dusty Merriman, who pitched eleven seasons in this league, answers.
# The pools below fill the time between plate appearances: who's up and what he's hitting, what
# the pitcher throws, how each man's season is going, tonight's storylines and two men talking.
#
# Whose voice opens a pool is fixed by its key: story, scout, tonight, duel and react pools are
# Dusty's; every other pool is Chip's. "||" splits a line into an exchange, the voices taking
# turns from that first one. Every topic is told once a game, and no line airs twice in one.
#
# Tokens, beside the usual {batter} {pitcher} {team} {fielding} {home} {away} {inningOrd}:
#   the batter's season   {avg} {hits} {ab} {hrs} {rbis} {sbs} ("eleven home runs")
#   the batter tonight    {tonight} ("two for three with a home run")
#   the pitcher's season  {wl} ("7 and 3") {era} {pks} ("112 strikeouts") {pAvg} (his batting average)
#   the pitcher tonight   {nks} ("six strikeouts") {runsWord}
#   his stuff             {pitch} (his out pitch: "slider") {pitch2} {velo} (his top speed) {delivery}
#   the table             {record} (batting side) {fieldRecord} {hrLeader} {hrLead} {avgLeader}
#                         {avgLead} {sbLeader} {sbLead} {kLeader} {kLead}
#   a defensive play      {fielderName}
#   the booth             {chip} {dusty}
# =============================================================================

# --- The man coming up. Early in the season, before the numbers mean anything: no stats.
::lines up.early
{batter} steps in for the {team}.
Here's {batter}. Not enough at-bats yet this season to tell you anything true about him.
Now batting for the {team}, {batter}.
{batter} digs in. Early days, small numbers.
{batter} to the plate. Still getting the feel of the season, same as the rest of us.
{batter} up for the {team}. We'll know a lot more about him in a month.
::endlines

# --- His first time up tonight, with his season.
::lines up.first
{batter} steps in, hitting {avg} with {hrs} and {rbis}.
Here's {batter}, a {avg} hitter on the year, with {hrs}.
{batter} to the plate for the {team}. He's at {avg}, {hrs}, {rbis}.
{batter} up now. {avg} on the season.
Now batting, {batter}. {avg}, {hrs}, {sbs}.
{batter} digs in. {hits} hits in {ab} at-bats this season. That's {avg}.
The {team} send up {batter}. {avg}, with {rbis}.
{batter} takes his practice cuts and steps in. {avg} hitter, {hrs}.
Here comes {batter}, {avg} with {rbis} on the year.
{batter} to bat. He comes in hitting {avg}.
::endlines

# --- Back up again, with his night so far.
::lines up.again
{batter} again. He's {tonight} so far tonight.
Here's {batter}, {tonight} on the night.
{batter} back in the box. {tonight} tonight.
{batter}, who's {tonight} this evening.
{batter} steps in. Tonight he's {tonight}.
Back comes {batter}. {tonight}.
::endlines

# --- Back up again, with his night and his season (only once he has a season to speak of).
::lines up.again.season
{batter} back up. {tonight} tonight, {avg} on the year.
Here's {batter} again. {avg} hitter, {tonight} tonight.
{batter} steps back in. {tonight} tonight, and {hrs} on the season.
::endlines

::lines up
Here's {batter}.
{batter} to the plate.
{batter} steps in for the {team}.
::endlines

# --- The situation, once an inning when it calls for it.
::lines sit.risp
Runner in scoring position, and {batter} has a chance to do something about it.
Man in scoring position for the {team}. {pitcher} needs a big pitch here.
They've got a runner where a single scores him. {batter} knows it. So does everybody else.
Scoring position. This is the at-bat the {team} have been waiting on. || It's the one {pitcher} has been dreading.
A run's in reach, and {batter} has the bat in his hands.
Somebody's in scoring position. {pitcher} looks in for the sign and takes a long breath.
::endlines

::lines sit.loaded
Bases loaded. Nowhere to put him.
The bags are full, and {pitcher} has to throw strikes now. || He has to. A walk's a run.
Bases loaded for {batter}. The crowd's up. The ones who can stand, anyway.
Bases loaded, and {batter} has a chance to clear them.
Every bag taken. {pitcher} steps off and rubs up the ball. || Take your time, son. They're not going anywhere.
::endlines

::lines sit.late
Late, and close. This is why you stayed.
{inningOrd} inning, a run in it either way. Every pitch counts from here.
Late in a tight one. || I can't sit still for these, {chip}. I never could. || You pitched in them. || That's why I can't sit still.
Close game, late innings. The vendors have stopped walking. Even they want to see this.
Here we go. Late, close, and nobody in the park is talking. || Except us. || Except us.
::endlines

::lines sit.blowout
It's a lopsided one, but they play nine. They always play nine.
Big lead, and the bleachers are thinning out. The ones who stay are the ones with nowhere warmer to be.
Not much doubt about this one. || There's always doubt, {chip}. I saw a team blow a nine-run lead in this park. || What happened? || The lights went out for twenty minutes and they came back on cold.
They're playing it out. Somebody's going to have a nice line in the box score out of this.
A blowout, but it still counts the same in the standings. Ask anybody who finished a game back.
::endlines

# --- The changeover: the starters, the leaders, the table, the stretch, the drag.
::lines break.starter
On the mound for the {fielding}, {pitcher}. He's {wl} this season with a {era} earned run average.
{pitcher} takes the hill for the {fielding}. {wl}, {era}, {pks}.
Pitching for the {fielding} tonight, {pitcher}, at {wl} on the year.
{pitcher} gets the ball for the {fielding}. His earned run average coming in is {era}.
The {fielding} hand the ball to {pitcher}. {pks} on the season.
::endlines

::lines break.starter.new
On the mound for the {fielding}, {pitcher}. First start of the season for him.
{pitcher} takes the ball for the {fielding}. We haven't seen him pitch yet this year.
{pitcher} is the {fielding} starter, making his first appearance on the mound this season.
::endlines

::lines break.leaders.hr
Around the league, {hrLeader} leads everybody with {hrLead} home runs.
Home run race: {hrLeader} out in front with {hrLead}. || He's not missing mistakes. Hang one to him and it's gone.
{hrLeader} has {hrLead} home runs, most in the league. || And he hits them high. They come down with snow on them.
::endlines

::lines break.leaders.avg
The batting race: {avgLeader} is hitting {avgLead}, best in the league.
{avgLeader} leads the league at {avgLead}. || He just puts the bat on the ball. Every time. It's annoying to pitch to.
Top of the batting table, {avgLeader}, at {avgLead}.
::endlines

::lines break.leaders.sb
On the bases, {sbLeader} leads the league with {sbLead} steals.
{sbLeader} has stolen {sbLead} bags this season. || Catchers see him get on and start sweating.
::endlines

::lines break.leaders.k
On the mound, {kLeader} leads the league with {kLead} strikeouts.
Strikeout leader is {kLeader}, with {kLead} of them. || And he looks bored doing it, {chip}. Watch his face sometime.
{kLeader} has {kLead} strikeouts, tops in the league.
::endlines

::lines break.standings
The {team} come in at {record}. The {fielding} at {fieldRecord}.
Quick look at the table: {team} {record}, {fielding} {fieldRecord}.
{team}, {record}. {fielding}, {fieldRecord}. || Long season. Nobody's out of it yet. || Some of them are out of it. || Nobody's out of it on the air.
::endlines

::lines break.stretch
Seventh-inning stretch. Everybody up. The organist's playing the song. It's the one song.
Middle of the seventh, and the crowd's on its feet. Most of them, anyway. Section eleven's seats bolt to the floor and the people in them stay down.
Time for the stretch. || I stretch every inning now, {chip}. My back went eleven seasons ago and never came home.
Stretch time at the Hughson. The organist's warming up. The crowd sings along, about half a beat behind him.
::endlines

::lines break.drag
Middle of the fifth. The grounds crew are out with the drags. Best-run part of the whole operation.
The crew are dragging the infield. Four of them, one mat each, and they've been doing it since before most of these players were born.
Infield drag in the middle of the fifth. || That crew are the best defenders in the building. || They don't make errors? || They don't make errors where anybody can see.
::endlines

# --- Scouting the hitter, by type: power, contact, glove, neither; and his legs.
::lines scout.power
{batter} is a power bat. He's up there to hit it a long way, and he'll take his strikeouts to do it.
Big swing on {batter}. You can get him to chase, but miss over the plate and he hits it into next week.
{batter} sells out for power. Every swing's a home run swing. Some of them are even home runs.
With {batter} you pitch carefully. He doesn't need to square it up to hit it out.
You don't beat {batter} with a fastball in. He's been waiting for that pitch since he was eleven.
{batter} swings hard enough to hurt himself. || Has he? || Twice. Both times he hit it out first.
::endlines

::lines scout.contact
{batter} is a contact hitter. Short swing, puts it in play, makes the defence work.
{batter} won't strike out much. He fouls off the tough ones and waits for one he can poke somewhere.
Slap hitter. {batter} goes the other way better than anybody on this club.
You can't overpower {batter}. He'll keep fouling them back till you make a mistake.
{batter} chokes up two inches on the bat. Doesn't hit it far. Doesn't miss it either.
{batter} sprays it all over the field. You can't shift on him. || You can't shift on anybody here, {dusty}. The outfield's a bog. || That's half the reason.
::endlines

::lines scout.glove
{batter} is in the lineup for his glove. Anything hit near him is an out.
They play {batter} for the defence. Whatever he hits is a bonus.
Best glove on the {team}, {batter}. Whatever he does at the plate, he saves them a run or two most nights.
{batter} could field a ground ball in a dust storm. People say he has. || Has he? || I was there. I couldn't see a thing. He came out with the ball.
The bat's ordinary. {batter} earns his rations in the field.
{batter} has soft hands. A hard grounder hits his glove and just stops, like it was tired.
::endlines

::lines scout.balanced
{batter} does a bit of everything. Hits for some average, some pop, catches the ball.
No one thing stands out on {batter}. Managers love a man like that.
{batter} is a steady player. He shows up, he plays, he doesn't hurt you anywhere.
{batter} is the kind of player you notice at the end of a season. Good numbers, every column.
::endlines

::lines scout.speed
And {batter} can run. Put the ball on the ground and he's a problem.
Watch {batter}'s legs. Anything in the infield, he's got a chance to beat it out.
{batter} is one of the fastest men in the league. If he gets on, the pitcher has to think about him.
::endlines

# --- Scouting the pitcher: his style, his delivery, his tempo.
::lines scout.pitch.flamethrower
{pitcher} throws hard. Fastball up to {velo}, and he'll keep throwing it till you prove you can hit it.
This is a power arm. {pitcher} lives on the fastball and puts guys away up in the zone with it.
{pitcher} tops out around {velo}. He's got a {pitch2}, but mostly he just dares you.
You want velocity, {pitcher} is your man. {velo} on a good night. || And on a bad night? || {velo}, and nobody knows where it's going.
{pitcher} is all arm. Big fastball, and the catcher's hand is purple by the fifth.
::endlines

::lines scout.pitch.sinkerballer
{pitcher} is a sinkerball pitcher. He wants the ball on the ground and the infield busy.
Everything {pitcher} throws moves down. Expect ground balls, plenty of them.
{pitcher} doesn't strike out many. He gets the grounder, and the double play when there's one on. || Smart pitcher. Saves his arm. || I pitched like that. || You pitched like that because you couldn't throw ninety. || That's also true.
With {pitcher} the infielders had better be awake. The sinker gets beaten into the dirt all night.
{pitcher} throws the sinker most of the time and the {pitch} when he needs a strikeout.
::endlines

::lines scout.pitch.slider
{pitcher} has a wipeout slider. It starts at the hitter's belt and finishes at his shoe tops.
Slider's the pitch with {pitcher}. He'll throw it in any count.
{pitcher} sets up the slider with the fastball. Once he's ahead, here comes the slider.
{pitcher} throws that slider about four times in ten. Hitters know it's coming. They swing over it anyway.
::endlines

::lines scout.pitch.junkballer
{pitcher} doesn't throw hard. Changeup, curveball, a fastball in the mid-eighties. He gets by on nerve.
{pitcher} is a junkballer, and I mean that kindly. He changes speeds and lets hitters get themselves out.
His fastball wouldn't break a window. {pitcher} still wins, because nobody's ever on time against him.
{pitcher} throws the changeup about a third of the time. Hitters swing at it and miss by a foot.
Watch the hitters' front knees against {pitcher}. They buckle on the changeup every time.
::endlines

::lines scout.pitch.splitter
{pitcher}'s got the splitter. Looks like a fastball, then the bottom drops out of it.
Watch for the split-finger from {pitcher} with two strikes. It dives at the plate.
{pitcher} throws the fastball and the splitter, and they look the same out of his hand for most of the way.
{pitcher} holds the splitter between two fingers spread wide. He's got hands like a pair of shovels.
::endlines

::lines scout.pitch.curveball
{pitcher} has a big curveball. Twelve to six. The kind they used to put in instruction books.
{pitcher} gets ahead with the fastball and finishes with the curve. Batters know it's coming and can't do much about it.
That curveball of {pitcher}'s is the best in the league. || I'd say top three. || Who's better? || Nobody, but I don't like to give out first place.
{pitcher}'s curve comes in at the hitter's eyes and drops into the zone. Half of them are taken for strike three.
::endlines

::lines scout.pitch.cutter
{pitcher} throws a cutter most of the time. It breaks late, a few inches, right onto the hands.
The cut fastball is {pitcher}'s pitch. He breaks bats with it. The clubs keep a pile of them by the dugout.
{pitcher} works off the cutter. Left-handers get it in on the fists all night.
::endlines

::lines scout.pitch.knuckleballer
{pitcher} throws a knuckleball. Sixty-odd miles an hour, no spin, and nobody knows where it's going. Including him.
Knuckleballer on the mound. {pitcher} could pitch till he's sixty with that thing. || Can the catcher? || The catcher's already retiring. He just doesn't know it.
{pitcher}'s knuckler doesn't rotate. It floats in and moves at the last second. The catcher wears the big mitt for him.
Hitting a knuckleball is like swatting a moth with a broom. {pitcher} has been throwing them for years.
{pitcher} tops out at {velo} on the fastball, and he throws it once an inning to remind them he has one.
::endlines

::lines scout.delivery.over
{pitcher} comes straight over the top. That's where the downward angle comes from.
Classic over-the-top delivery on {pitcher}. Tall and straight, like a man pulling down a window.
::endlines

::lines scout.delivery.threequarter
{pitcher} throws from three-quarters. Nothing unusual about it, and that's how he likes it.
Three-quarters delivery on {pitcher}, clean and repeatable. He could do it asleep.
::endlines

::lines scout.delivery.sidearm
{pitcher} drops down and comes sidearm. Right-handers hate it. The ball comes from behind them.
Sidearm delivery on {pitcher}. Everything runs, and nothing comes from where you expect.
::endlines

::lines scout.delivery.submarine
{pitcher} is a submariner. His knuckles nearly scrape the mound. The ball rises on the way in.
That's a submarine delivery. {pitcher} throws from somewhere around his ankles. || How does he do it? || Bad back and a good attitude.
::endlines

::lines scout.tempo.quick
{pitcher} works fast. Gets the ball, gets the sign, throws. The game moves when he's pitching.
Quick tempo from {pitcher}. Hitters barely have time to dig in.
::endlines

::lines scout.tempo.quick.runner
With a man on, watch {pitcher}'s slide step. He's as quick to the plate as anybody. Runners don't go on him.
{pitcher} is quick to the plate. That runner at first will think twice.
::endlines

::lines scout.tempo.slow
{pitcher} takes his time. Walks round the mound, rubs up the ball, looks in. Every pitch.
Slow worker, {pitcher}. You could make a sandwich between pitches. || I have.
::endlines

::lines scout.tempo.slow.runner
{pitcher} has a big leg kick, and runners love it. Watch the man on first.
With {pitcher}, that big leg kick gives runners a head start. The catcher hates it.
::endlines

# --- Storylines: the season against what a man is supposed to be.
::lines story.leader.hr
{batter} has {hrs}, at or near the top of the league. Pitchers know exactly who he is.
Not many in the league have more home runs than {batter}. {hrs}.
{batter} is in the home run race with {hrs}. || Does he think about it? || Everybody thinks about it. The good ones don't talk about it.
::endlines

::lines story.leader.avg
{batter} is hitting {avg}. That's up with the best in the league.
{avg} for {batter}. He's in the hunt for the batting title.
::endlines

::lines story.leader.sb
{batter} has {sbs} this season, up with anybody in the league.
{sbs} for {batter}. If he gets on, he's going. Everybody knows it, and he still makes it.
::endlines

::lines story.leader.k
{pitcher} has {pks} this season. That's up with the league's best.
{pks} for {pitcher}. Hitters walk back to the dugout shaking their heads against him.
::endlines

::lines story.power.slump
{batter} has {hrs} this year. For a man with his power, that's a long dry spell.
They put {batter} in the middle of the order to hit home runs, and he's got {hrs}. You can see it in his swing. He's pressing.
{batter}'s hitting {avg} with {hrs}. || That's not what he's paid for. || He's paid in rations, {chip}. || Then he's not earning his rations.
The power's gone missing for {batter}. {hrs}. One good swing would change his whole month.
{batter} has been staying late in the cage every night. {hrs} all year. || It'll come. || It had better come soon.
::endlines

::lines story.power.hot
{batter} has {hrs} already. He's on one of those runs where every mistake leaves the park.
{hrs} for {batter}. When he's like this you pitch around him and take your chances with the next man.
{batter} is seeing it big right now. {hrs}, {rbis}.
::endlines

::lines story.power.surprise
{batter} was never a home run hitter, and he's got {hrs}. Nobody saw that coming.
{batter} never hit for power in his life, and he's sitting on {hrs}. || What changed? || He says he started eating breakfast. || That's it? || He says it was a big breakfast.
{hrs} for {batter}. That's more than some of the big swingers in this league. Short swing, and the ball just carries.
::endlines

::lines story.contact.hot
{batter} is hitting {avg}. Singles, doubles, a bunt now and then. He just doesn't make outs.
{avg} for {batter}. That's what a contact hitter looks like when everything falls in.
{batter} at {avg}. || I'd have hated pitching to him. || You hated pitching to everybody. || I hated him more.
::endlines

::lines story.contact.cold
{batter} is a contact man hitting {avg}. He's putting the ball in play. It's been finding gloves.
Tough year for {batter}. {avg}. He hits it hard enough. Right at people.
{batter} at {avg}. For a hitter like him, that's hard to look at. || He'll come out of it. They always do. || Not always. || He'll come out of it.
::endlines

::lines story.glove.hitting
{batter} is in there for the glove, and he's hitting {avg}. The {team} will take that every day.
{avg} from {batter}. His manager put him in for defence and now can't take him out.
::endlines

::lines story.glove.cold
{batter} is hitting {avg}, but nobody plays him for the bat. He's saved more runs than he's cost.
{avg} for {batter}. The {team} don't care. You should see him in the field.
::endlines

::lines story.hot
{batter} is swinging it well. {avg}, {hrs}, {rbis}.
{avg} for {batter}. He's in a good place right now.
::endlines

::lines story.cold
{batter} has had a hard season. {avg}. He's still out there every day.
{batter} at {avg}. Some years go like that.
::endlines

::lines story.speed.running
{batter} has {sbs}. Get him on and he's standing on second before the catcher's out of his crouch.
{sbs} this year for {batter}. Pitchers throw over to first more for him than for anybody.
::endlines

::lines story.speed.idle
{batter} is one of the quickest men in the league and he hasn't stolen a base all year. || Why not? || Nobody's asked him to.
Funny thing about {batter}. He can fly, and he's got {sbs}. He doesn't like to run.
::endlines

::lines story.pitcher.ace
{pitcher} has been the best arm on the {fielding}. {era} earned run average, {wl}.
{pitcher} comes in with an earned run average of {era}. Runs are hard to come by against him.
::endlines

::lines story.pitcher.rough
It's been a hard year for {pitcher}. His earned run average is {era}. He keeps getting the ball, though.
{pitcher}'s earned run average is {era}. || That's a big number. || It's a long season. He'll bring it down.
::endlines

::lines story.pitcher.winning
{pitcher} is {wl} this season. He's been winning, whatever the rest of the numbers say.
{wl} for {pitcher}. His club scores runs for him, and he takes them.
::endlines

::lines story.pitcher.losing
{pitcher} is {wl} this year. Some of that's on him, and some of it's the bats behind him going quiet.
{wl} for {pitcher}. || Pitcher wins are a funny stat, {chip}. || He'd still rather have more of them. || We all would.
::endlines

# This league deals its men to clubs and positions game by game, so a man on the mound tonight
# may have played left field on Tuesday.
::lines story.twoway
Here's the thing about {pitcher}. He's hitting {pAvg} this season, and tonight he's pitching. || That's this league. You get dealt a card and you play it.
{pitcher} played the outfield last week. Tonight he's on the mound. He's hitting {pAvg}, if anyone's keeping track.
This league doesn't do positions the way the old one did. {pitcher} pitches tonight. Some nights he hits. He's at {pAvg} with a bat.
::endlines

